    "body": "2026 Paris Motor Show • OMODA 4 Lands in Paris To Showcase Futuristic Styling, Looks Like a Discount Urus\nThis Citroen Relay Is a Swamper-Style Camper Van With a Cozy Oak Interior\n22 photos\nPhoto: YouTube Screenshot / Van life deployed\nLately, I've been looking at more swamper-style vehicles, and I fell in love with the look. Today, I have a swamper camper van that blends a cool exterior look with much-needed creature comforts. What makes this project even more impressive is the fact that it's a DIY creation.\n\"What's a swamper?\" you might be wondering. It's an off-road-inspired conversion of a standard passenger or panel vehicle. It's built to look like a tough overland vehicle – some even act like it, although most just have the looks.\nWhat we have here is a conversion based on a Citroen Relay with a long wheelbase and a high roof. It was created by a man known online on YouTube as \"Van life deployed.\"\nAs usual, let's start the tour with the exterior. This Relay is certainly an eye-catcher due to its unique look. First off, it's finished in black and white – the lower rocker panels, the bumper, part of the roof, and the hood are black. The rest of the van minus some mods is white. What's more, the van rides on Extreme Alloy wheels wrapped in Comforser CF110 all-terrain tires.\nPhoto: YouTube Screenshot / Van life deployed At the rear, you'll discover a ladder that offers quick and easy access to the roof. There, the builder installed a roof rack holding two 175 W solar panels, a two-in-one antenna (for TV and Wi-Fi), and a Fiamma F80S awning measuring 3.7 meters (12 feet) in length. It also offers plenty of free space to add other accessories or gear.\nOther notable mods include side steps, four windows (two on each side), and power and water connections.\nSlide the door open, and you'll be greeted by a beautifully built interior. It really feels like a tiny studio apartment, especially since it has a closed layout and you can't see the cabin. By closed layout, I mean that there's a wall separating the driver's cabin from the living space.\nPhoto: YouTube Screenshot / Van life deployed Regarding aesthetics, you'll notice a variety of oak elements including oak slat walls and oak cabinetry, grey laminated cabinet faces, grey carpeted flooring, and a white ceiling.\nThe divider wall I mentioned earlier integrates a tiny window and an overhead storage unit with a spacious headliner shelf, a shelf with a lip, and a small cabinet.\nNext to the same wall, the builders added one of the two kitchen units. It offers a dual-burner induction stovetop, a bit of surface area for meal prepping, three drawers, and a cabinet.\nPhoto: YouTube Screenshot / Van life deployed Straight ahead as you enter, you'll come across the bathroom. It boasts a shower with a handheld wand, two wall niches for storage, a Trelino composting toilet, and an overhead cabinet with a mirror. What's more, a Maxxair Dome vent fan draws out moisture and nasty smells, while a retractable door prevents any water from splashing outside.\nThe other part of the kitchen is on the same side as the bathroom, further inside the camper. It has a deep black stainless steel sink, a 95-liter fridge with a tiny freezer compartment, two outlets, and a wall spice rack. There's also a cabinet underneath the sink that houses a water filtration system.\nOn the opposite side, you'll discover an overhead cabinet, a tea shelf, and a counter with a top. The counter hides the rig's power system – its highlights are a 300 Ah lithium iron phosphate battery bank and a 3,000 W inverter.\nPhoto: YouTube Screenshot / Van life deployed You can use three charging sources: shore power, solar power , and DC-DC charging while the engine is running. Basically, it offers all you need to be able to travel off-grid without worries – you just have to make sure you have enough water, food, and fuel.\nNext to the overhead cabinet I mentioned above, you'll find an inverter controller and a battery monitor.\nThe rear part of the living space is reserved for seating and sleeping. The day setup consists of two benches and a table in between. This area sits a bit higher than the rest of the interior, and the extra height was put to good use thanks to a floor compartment.\nPhoto: YouTube Screenshot / Van life deployed Two cabinets and an overhead shelf above form a \"U\" shape and offer additional storage space. The benches have hinged tops that can be lifted to reveal more room. However, the driver-side bench also houses the freshwater tank and a Truma Combi heater that heats both air and water.\nYou'll also notice charging points, two reading lights, and a TV attached to a wall-mounted swivel. A nice and clever touch is that the van's converter added an Alexa system and connected it to the RGB lights in the back of the van, so you can control them by using voice commands.\nCreating the sleeping arrangement is easier than you'd think. You must drop the table to the same height as the benches to connect them, add extra panels to fulfill the same purpose as the dropped table, and rearrange the cushions.\nPhoto: YouTube Screenshot / Van life deployed As for plumbing, the van is equipped with a 100-liter (26-gallon) freshwater tank and a 55-liter (14.5-gallon) under-mounted greywater tank with a drain tap.\nAll in all, this guy created a well-equipped home on wheels that efficiently uses the little interior real estate available, all hidden behind a cool swamper-style exterior.\nHe ended up selling the van, but he shared that a similar spec could cost around £45,000 to £55,000 ($59,585 to $72,826) depending on mileage and vehicle specs, as well as how greedy the builders are.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\ncamper van Camper van life conversion off-grid Swamper Citroen Relay DIY\nAbout the author: Mircea Mazuru\nStarting out with a motorcycle permit just because he could get one two years earlier than a driver's license, Mircea keeps his passion for bikes (motor or no motor) alive to this day. His lifelong dream is to build his own custom camper van. Full profile\nUP NEXT New BMW M350 xDrive Gets a Virtual Tuning Job, Do You Dig the Sportier Design?",
